      "narrative": "The copper lamps flicker as you turn to Mara Venn. She's been nursing the same half-empty mug since you arrived, her charcoal coat shedding beads of rainwater onto the scarred floorboards. When you ask about the Ash Gate, her sharp eyes narrow \u2014 not with suspicion, but with the look of someone deciding how much to say.\n\nShe sets the mug down with a soft clink.\n\n\"The Ash Gate.\" She repeats the name like it has weight. \"I've been poking around that thing for three weeks now. Closed the night Old Bram died \u2014 you heard about that, I assume? Ferryman keeled over right at the base of it. Heart gave out, they said.\"\n\nShe leans forward, elbows on the table. Her voice drops.\n\n\"Here's what I *can* tell you. The lock mechanism \u2014 that big iron wheel set into the arch \u2014 it's not broken. Toran kept it oiled and true. But something's jammed it from the *other* side. I've tried everything short of blasting powder. The wheel turns a quarter rotation, then binds like it's caught on bone.\"\n\nShe pauses, frowning. Her thumb traces the rim of her mug.\n\n\"I was going to ask Toran about the internal assembly today, actually. He built the thing. Might know what's back there that could seize up like that.\"\n\nShe looks at you expectantly \u2014 but something flickers in her expression. A hesitation. She's holding something back. You can feel it, like a word caught in her throat that she decided not to speak.\n\n*What was she about to say?* The question sits unanswered between you.\n\nMara clears her throat. \"Anyway. That's what I've got. Toran's through the back if you want his side of it. Or... was there something else you were hoping I'd know?\""
    },
