      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch groans shut behind you, cutting off the warmth of the tavern's hearth. Morning air hits you \u2014 damp, carrying the smell of wet stone and something older, like turned earth after a long drought breaks.\n\nThe street is narrow, cobbled, lined with shuttered windows and the occasional rain barrel. You follow Mara's directions: past the tanner's with its hanging hides, left at the cracked well, and then the buildings fall away.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands alone.\n\nIt rises from bare packed earth at the end of a short dirt path \u2014 a cracked black arch maybe fifteen feet tall, its stone veined with pale lines like scars. No wall flanks it, no building supports it. It simply *stands*, a doorway to nowhere, framing a patch of grey sky and the distant slope of a hill. At its base, the ground is scuffed and dark, a patch of stained earth where Old Bram fell.\n\nUp close, you see the mechanism Mara described: an iron wheel as wide as your shoulders, set into the right pillar, its spokes crusted with rust but still bearing the gleam of fresh oil near the hub. A heavy chain loops through the wheel's spokes, padlocked to itself \u2014 Mara's doing, likely, to keep anyone from forcing it.\n\nThe air here is still. No birds call. No wind moves.\n\nThe arch's interior shimmers faintly \u2014 a distortion like heat haze, though the morning is cool. Beyond it, through that shimmer, you can see the hill and the sky, utterly ordinary. Whatever the Gate does, it's dormant now.\n\nThe stained earth at your feet. The locked chain. The silent arch.\n\nWhat do you do?"
    },
