      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door admits you with the same groan it gave on your departure. The warmth wraps around you again \u2014 hearth-fire, old tallow, the sharp tang of spilled ale soaking into floorboards that have seen decades of boots.\n\nYour table stands where you left it. Scarred oak, a notch on the left edge from some forgotten blade or flung tankard, one leg shimmed with a folded square of leather. A guttering candle stub sits in a pool of its own wax.\n\nToran Vex glances up from behind the bar, a rag in one hand, and recognition flickers across his face. He gives a short nod \u2014 not welcoming, not hostile. Neutral. The nod of a man who's noted your presence and filed it away.\n\nThe tavern is quieter now than it was. A single patron sits near the cold back wall: a round-shouldered man in a patched wool cloak, nursing a small cup, staring at nothing. He doesn't look up.\n\nThe fire pops.\n\nYour chair scrapes as you pull it out and sit."
